# The judgment record. A concern is a question the substrate could not settle
# by citation alone; a ruling is the adjudicator's decision, attributed and
# dated: ogc:rulingText states it in the register's own words, and
# ogc:verbatim keeps the message as sent, untouched (R-47).
# Hand-edited source graph; docs/rulings.md is rendered from it.

rul:Z a prov:Agent, prov:Person ; rdfs:label "Z (Michael Zargham), adjudicator" .
rul:Julie a prov:Agent, prov:Person ; rdfs:label "Julie Hollek (Humane Intelligence), co-author" .

rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "plan-mode walkthrough of the open concerns, 2026-09-05 (Claude Code AskUserQuestion popups; Z selected one option per concern)" ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith rul:Z ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concerns

rul:C-01 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "test coverage: abstract tests vs concrete applications" ;
    ogc:problem """The paper draft conflates tests (abstract) that cover requirements with the concrete applications of those tests during a case, which produce the evidence. Z's comment on the draft (2026-09-05): "it seems to conflate tests (abstract) with (concrete) applications of those tests ... test coverage ... relates the requirements we carry about to tests (abstract) that cover them; Not the specific incidents (plural) of those tests applied during a specific case. the latter produces the evidence which is ultimately rolled up to support validating (or invalidating) the hypothesis that the requirement is met." SEVOCAB carries ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021 3.28 test coverage, which supports the abstract reading.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z's comment on the paper draft; SEVOCAB entry check" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:test-coverage ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-02 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "operating environment: which canonical anchor" ;
    ogc:problem """Term contract v5 anchors "operating environment" to the automotive Operational Design Domain (SAE J3016, SAE J3259, ISO 34503), all paid sources outside the evaluation domain. SEVOCAB carries "operational environment" (IEEE 982-2024 3.1): physical context, setting, and circumstances used to support the in-service operation of a system.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "term contract v5 review against SEVOCAB" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:operational-environment ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-03 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "operational envelope: no canonical entry" ;
    ogc:problem """Neither ISO 9000:2026 nor SEVOCAB defines "operational envelope". Term contract v5 cites process-safety usage (CCPS "safe operating envelope"), a weak fit. Functionally the envelope is the declared requirement set with its acceptance criteria and weights for the operational environment.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "term contract v5 review against ISO 9000:2026 and SEVOCAB" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:operational-envelope ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-04 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "redistribution of source snapshots" ;
    ogc:problem """SEBoK v2.14 is CC BY-NC-SA; the ISO 9000:2026 OBP preview is copyrighted text captured as 37 screenshots. Committing either to a public repository is a licence exposure; not committing them weakens machine verification of quotes in CI.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "source register design" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-05 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "deployment sensitivity: refined term or coinage" ;
    ogc:problem """The weight on each acceptance criterion by consequence of failure. SEVOCAB carries risk-based testing (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021 3.16). Is "deployment sensitivity" a refinement of that or a fourth coinage.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "coinage audit of term contract v5" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:deployment-sensitivity ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-06 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "IRI base: w3id now or Pages URL" ;
    ogc:problem """Ontology and record IRIs need a stable base. w3id.org/og-caie requires a PR to perma-id/w3id.org and resolves nowhere until merged; the GitHub Pages URL resolves immediately but renaming later breaks every Turtle file.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "repository design" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-07 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "scope: requirements and interfaces, or architecture too" ;
    ogc:problem """Z's SEBoK screenshots draw the scope box around Concept Definition and System Requirements Definition (including Assess Interactions across Interfaces) and stop before Logical and Physical Architecture. The infra drawing (frontend, backend, data) is a realization sketch. Do part and port defs stay at the interface level or allocate to that drawing.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "SEBoK v2.14 figures annotated by Z" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:C-08 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "adequacy vs appropriateness" ;
    ogc:problem """The paper draft and term contract v5 label the attestation's context judgment "adequacy". The primary source for the pairing (Hawkins, Kelly, Knight and Graydon 2011, assurance claim points) uses "appropriateness" for asserted context and "sufficiency" for asserted solution; ISA 500 uses appropriateness (quality) and sufficiency (quantity). "Adequacy" also collides with ISO 9000:2026 3.11.2 review.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z's correction during planning; verified against Hawkins et al. 2011 sections 3.2 and 3.3 and ISA 500 paragraphs 5(b), 5(f), A8" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:appropriateness ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:sufficiency ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- rulings

rul:R-01 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 1 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-01 ;
    ogc:rulingText "SEVOCAB test coverage (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021 3.28) is adopted. The distinction between the abstract tests that cover requirements and the concrete applications of those tests during a case, which produce the evidence, is recorded as a scope note rather than as a term." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Adopt SEVOCAB test coverage; add the distinction as a note" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Glossary cites ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021 3.28 via SEVOCAB; scope note: acceptance criterion is the coverage item, probe is the test case, evidence is the concrete application. Paper asked to align." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-02 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 2 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-02 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The headword is operational environment, adopted from SEVOCAB (IEEE 982-2024 3.1). Operating environment remains an alternative label; the automotive Operational Design Domain sources (SAE J3016, SAE J3259, ISO 34503) leave the canon." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Adopt SEVOCAB 'operational environment'" ;
    ogc:changeNote "prefLabel operational environment (IEEE 982-2024 via SEVOCAB); altLabel operating environment; ODD and SAE dropped from the canon." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-03 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 3 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-03 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Operational envelope is kept as a house label, defined by composition and anchored to ISO 9000:2026 3.5.1 requirement as a specialization, with requirement set as an alternative label. It is not counted as a coinage." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Keep as house label, define by composition, altLabel 'requirement set'" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Class refined; anchored to ISO 9000:2026 3.5.1 requirement with relation specializes; not counted as coinage. SysML item def RequirementSet." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-04 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 4 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-04 ;
    ogc:rulingText "SEBoK v2.14 and the ISO 9000:2026 screenshots are both held locally and not committed. The repository commits their hashes and digests only, and the checks verify the quotes against the digests." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Hold both locally; commit hashes + digests only" ;
    ogc:changeNote "sources.ttl posture heldLocally for SEBoK v2.14 and the ISO 9000:2026 screenshots; digests with quotes and locators committed; CI verifies quotes against digests with pinned counts." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-05 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 5 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-05 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Deployment sensitivity is a refined term that specializes SEVOCAB risk-based testing (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021 3.16). It is not a coinage." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Refined: specializes risk-based testing" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Anchor SEVOCAB risk-based testing (29119-2 3.16), relation specializes, altLabel risk weight. Coinage stays at three." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-06 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 6 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-06 ;
    ogc:rulingText "IRIs are authored under https://w3id.org/og-caie/ from the start. The GitHub Pages URL is noted as the fallback until the w3id redirect is merged." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Author against w3id now, note the Pages fallback" ;
    ogc:changeNote "All IRIs under https://w3id.org/og-caie/; PR to perma-id/w3id.org opened in S0; README states the Pages fallback until it merges." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-07 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 7 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-07 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The scope is requirements and interfaces; architecture is excluded. Part and port definitions stay at the interface level, and the infrastructure drawing is cited as a realization sketch with nothing allocated to it." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Yes: requirements + interfaces, no architecture" ;
    ogc:changeNote "SysML carries part, port, interface, action and requirement defs only; no allocation, no physical structure; the infra drawing is cited as a realization sketch." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-08 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 8 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-08 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The pairing is appropriateness and sufficiency. The earlier label adequacy was a misquotation of the primary source; the functional use was correct and only the label was wrong. The primary source is to be checked and cited correctly." ;
    ogc:verbatim "\"adequacy/sufficiency\" i believe i misquoted in a previous version its appropriateness and sufficiency check the primary source and cite correctly. functionally my use was correct, just mislabed" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Every artifact uses appropriateness (of the declared context) and sufficiency (of the evidence); citations to Hawkins et al. 2011 3.2/3.3 with ISA 500 5(b)/5(f)/A8 as seeAlso; paper and term contract v5 asked to align." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

# ------------------------------------------------ concerns added during S1

rul:C-09 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the coinage set: which terms do we own" ;
    ogc:problem """Term contract v5 tags probe/scenario, deployment sensitivity, DSO, EPO and CAIE as [CAIE] assemblies. Under the rule that terms are used, not owned, which of these are coinages the project must own, and which are refinements of standard terms.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "coinage audit of term contract v5 during planning" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:domain-specific-ontology ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:evaluation-process-ontology ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:og-caie ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:plan-walkthrough-2026-09-05 .

rul:R-09 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 9 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-09 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Coinage is confined to the project's novel contributions. The two primary coinages are Domain-Specific Ontology and Evaluation Process Ontology; the method is named either CAIE or Ontology-Grounded CAIE, abbreviated OG-CAIE." ;
    ogc:verbatim "we have two primary coinages \"Domain-Specific Ontology\" and \"Evaluation Process Ontology\" ... focusing coinage only where it directly relates to our novel contributions ... feels like we would either stick with \"CAIE\" or Ontology-Grounded CAIE, which would OG-CAIE" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Coinage is exactly three: Domain-Specific Ontology, Evaluation Process Ontology, OG-CAIE (the method name). Probe and deployment sensitivity are refined terms (R-05); operational envelope is a house label (R-03). Julie Hollek and Z agreed the DSO and EPO expansions in the paper comments (Mala Kumar's note, 2026-09-05)." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-10 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "system under test: weak canonical entry" ;
    ogc:problem """SEVOCAB's only entry for "system under test (SUT)" reads "parts of the CBSS to be tested" (ISO/IEC 14756:1999, a performance-measurement standard). The cleaner entries are "test item: work product to be tested" (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021) and "system-of-interest: system whose life cycle is under consideration" (ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2026). The paper says "system under test" throughout. Options: keep SUT as prefLabel with the weak quote (current), or make "test item" canonical with SUT as altLabel.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "SEVOCAB entry check while building the glossary" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:test-item .

# ------------------------------------------------ rulings taken 2026-09-06 (sheet 01 walkthrough)

rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "row-by-row verification of the pending ISO quotes against Z's screenshots, 2026-09-06" ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith rul:Z ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-11 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "technical expert: one kind or several on the evaluation team" ;
    ogc:problem """ISO 9000:2026 3.12.9 defines technical expert in the audit context as a person who provides specific knowledge or expertise to the audit team. The glossary had bound it only to the DomainExpert part. Who else on the evaluation team is a technical expert, and in what.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 6 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:technical-expert ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-10 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 10 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-11 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The world model has more than one kind of technical expert. The domain expert is a technical expert in the domain-specific ontology: a person who can attest to the appropriateness of the Domain-Specific Ontology for the particular case being evaluated, that is, whether this system under test passes these requirements in this operating environment. The red-teamers are also technical experts, though not in the auditing sense: their expertise is in AI evaluation; they perform the tests the EPO specifies, conditioned on the DSO the domain expert supplies, and their job is to assemble and interpret evidence. Domain experts may be called on to consult on the interpretation of the resulting evidence. Both domain experts and AI experts belong on the evaluation team." ;
    ogc:verbatim "in our world model we have more than one kind of technical expert. the DomainExpert is a technical expert in the domain-specific-ontology. someone who can attest to the appropriateness of the the Domain Specific Ontology for a particular Case being evaluated (does this system under test, pass these requirements in this operating environment). The redteamer are also technical experts but not in the auditing sense. their expertise is in AI evaluation and they know how to performing tests specified by the EPO conditioned on the domain expert supplied DSO, their job is to assemble and interpret evidence. the DomainExperts may be called to consult on the interpretation of the resulting evidence. in my view both domain experts and AI experts belong on the evaluation team." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Glossary: technical expert kept as the genus (ISO 9000:2026 3.12.9), narrative rewritten to name the two kinds on the evaluation team and what each is expert in; bound to both DomainExpert and Evaluator. Model: doc comments of DomainExpert and Evaluator state their expertise and the case (system under test, requirement set, operational environment) the appropriateness judgment is about." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-12 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "assessing the assessors" ;
    ogc:problem """Technical experts may in principle have mirrored counterparts who assess the assessment (second-party review, as in the June prototype's audit module). Does this specification model them.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 6 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-11 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 11 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-12 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Technical experts may in principle have mirrored counterparts who assess the assessment, but this document does not model assessing the assessors. The one plausible place for the idea is a closing call to action, offering the reader OG-CAIE or an audit of the reader's own AI evaluation practice against this specification." ;
    ogc:verbatim "in principle they may have mirrored counterparts who assess the assessment but for the purpose of this document i don't think we want to get into assessing the assessors. except plausible as a final call to action offering the reader OG-CAIE or an audit of the readers AI eval practice against our OG-CAIE spec." ;
    ogc:changeNote "No assessor-of-assessors parts or shapes. The front page closes with a call to action: use OG-CAIE, or have an AI evaluation practice audited against this specification." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-13 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the chain from requirement to attestation: is the test plan explicit" ;
    ogc:problem """The record linked requirement to criterion, probe to criterion, run to probe, evidence to run, and attestation to criterion and evidence. It had no test plan node, no expected result on the criterion, and no binding from the attestation to the probe whose run produced its evidence, so the interpretation was not mechanically tied to the plan that produced what it interpreted.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 7 of rulings sheet 01 (requirement), Z's question" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:requirement ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:acceptance-criteria ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:attestation ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-12 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 12 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-13 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Traceability from the requirement, as a hypothesis that may be falsified, through an epistemically valid process that collects and interprets evidence to render the judgement, is critical for requirements to be useful concepts. The existing definitions are not to be overwritten. The record must carry a chain of evidence connecting the test plans to the evidence they would produce, the implementation of those plans that produces the evidence, the interpretation of that evidence, and the justified attestation that a requirement is or is not met, bound to that evidence and to the test plan that produced it. This is the level of abstraction at which the abstract EPO is encoded." ;
    ogc:verbatim "in my experience the traceability from the requirement (as hypothesis that may be falsified) and the epistemically valid process that collects and interprets evidence to render the judgement is critical for requirements to be useful concepts. i do not want you to overwrite the definitions i want you to ensure we have a chain of evidence connecting test plans to the evidence they would produce to the implementation of those plans to that produces evidence to the interpretation of that evidence to make a justified attestation that a requirement is or is not met bound to that evidence and the test plan that produced it. this is the level of abstraction at which we are encoding our abstract EPO." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Added, never overwriting: glossary terms test plan and expected results (SEVOCAB, 29119); seeAlso test execution on test, test result on attestation, test scenario (29119-11) on scenario. EPO: TestPlan (objectives, means), expectedResult on the criterion, underPlan from attestation to probe. Record: the plan, expected results on a1-a3, both attestations bound to probe-1, recommendation derived from the plan. Shapes: S2 criterion requires an expected result; S3-TestPlan; S3 probe must be a plan's means; S6 chain rule (evidence used comes from a run of the bound probe which exercises the attested criterion); S8 recommendation names the plan. Counterexample attestation-off-plan. Model: TestPlan item, expectedResultStated, probeId on the attestation, SCI-04 and SCI-06 extended. Traceback query returns plan, probe and expected result." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-14 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "probe versus run: trajectories, sessions and state feedback policies" ;
    ogc:problem """The record has one probe, one run and one evidence, which hides a distinction Z draws: a probe is an individual prompt-response pair; a run is a sequence of prompt-response pairs forming a trajectory within a session; sessions are stateful and LLM-system behaviour is highly context sensitive, nonlinear in the sense that a small change in input can cause large changes in output, especially when trajectories are continued. Test plans are not limited to single probes: they support strategies that determine probes according to a state feedback policy (semantic state feedback). Z: this is the place where a small amount of extra definition drawn from control engineering, system identification and metrology as applied to dynamical systems has the most to gain; hunt for canonical sources, starting with NIST.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 8 of rulings sheet 01, Z's comment" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:probe ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:test-plan ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:test ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-13 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 13 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-14 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The eight-term package is adopted as proposed: session and dialogue (NIST); trajectory, with the output-feedback anchor for the policy (IEC 60050-351); test strategy (ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2); non-deterministic system (29119-11); repeatability, reproducibility and measurement uncertainty (VIM, NIST TN 1297). Session and Turn enter the record; a Strategy is a plan's means; the chain rule is extended accordingly." ;
    ogc:verbatim "Adopt the eight-term package as proposed: session, dialogue (NIST); trajectory, output-feedback anchor for the policy (IEC 60050-351); test strategy (29119-2); non-deterministic system (29119-11); repeatability, reproducibility, measurement uncertainty (VIM, NIST TN 1297). Session and Turn in the record; Strategy as a plan's means; chain rule extended." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Sources: IEC 60050-351 (Electropedia, cite-only, quotes pending), JCGM 200:2012 (held locally), NIST TN 1297 (committed). Glossary: eight terms; probe and test narratives tightened to one turn and to the session. EPO: Session, Turn, Trajectory, Strategy (a prov:Plan), inSession, turnIndex, policy, groundedIn; a TestPlan's means is a Probe or a Strategy. Record: session-1 with turn-1 and trajectory-1 replace run-1. Shapes: S4-Session, S4-Turn, S3-Strategy; S5 evidence from a turn; S6 chain rule through the turn; S3 probe belongs to a plan directly or through a strategy. Counterexample attestation-off-turn (two-turn session under a strategy). Model: Session item, sessionId and turnIndex on Evidence, SCI-05 extended. Traceback query returns session and turn." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-15 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "operational envelope: what it adds to a set of requirements" ;
    ogc:problem """R-03 kept operational envelope as a house label anchored to ISO 9000:2026 requirement. Is it merely the set of requirements, or does it carry more: the binding of those requirements to one system under test in one operational environment.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 8 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:operational-envelope ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-14 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 14 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-15 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The operational envelope refines the concept of requirements in its specificity: it conditions explicitly on an operating environment and on the system under test, stating that this system in this environment must meet these requirements. It is closely related to a set of requirements, but Contextual AI Evaluation expects different requirements for different systems under test in different operating environments. The distinction is deliberate: it separates the method from benchmarks and aligns it with the practice for safety-critical systems." ;
    ogc:verbatim "operational envelope refines the the concept of requirements in its specificity. an operational envelope explicit conditions on an operating environment and the system under test. we are saying THIS system in the THIS environment must meet THESE requirements. its closely related to set of requirements but we're really leaning into the Contextual AI Evaluation so we expect different requirements for different systems under test in different operating environments. this is explicitly to make this distinct from benchmarks are more like safety critical systems" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Anchor unchanged (ISO 9000:2026 3.5.1 requirement, specializes). Narrative and scope note rewritten. EPO: RequirementSet names its system under test (epo:systemUnderTest, an earl:TestSubject) as well as its environment; shape S2 requires both; SysML RequirementSet gains sutNamed and SCI-02 checks it; the measles record names chatbot v1." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-16 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "requirements traceability and metrological traceability" ;
    ogc:problem """ISO 9000:2026 3.5.11 Note 2 defers the metrological sense of traceability to ISO/IEC Guide 99 (the VIM). The glossary anchored requirements traceability to 29148 via SEVOCAB and kept metrology out. Should the metrological sense be visible, and how do the two relate.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 9 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:requirements-traceability ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-15 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 15 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-16 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Requirements traceability and metrological traceability are kept distinct. When both are done computationally with knowledge graphs they can be chained, because they are in principle the same notion at different levels of abstraction. A note records that computational instruments are used to measure computational systems, so the concept of metrological traceability still applies." ;
    ogc:verbatim "you can chain requirements traceability to metrological traceability when we do them both computationally using using knowldedge graphs because they are in principle the same at different levels of abstraction. so we want them to be kept distict but we should note that we're using computational instruments to measure computational systems so the metrological traceability concept still applies" ;
    ogc:changeNote "VIM 2.41 metrological traceability added as a seeAlso on requirements traceability with a scope note stating the relation; canonical anchor unchanged (SEVOCAB, 29148)." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-17 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "conformity, conformance, and what SHACL calls validation" ;
    ogc:problem """ISO 9000:2026 3.5.9 deprecates conformance as a synonym of conformity. W3C SHACL calls its machine check validation, which by this specification's V and V split is verification. The glossary had one term, conformity, and banned conformance. How to name the machine-checked, correctly constructed record without confusing it with fulfilment of a requirement in the ISO sense, or with validation.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 10 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:conformity ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:conformance ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-16 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 16 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-17 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Since ISO deprecates conformance, the word is reclaimed for the specific requirement that the record be correctly constructed according to the SHACL shapes, and conformity is used for fulfilment of a requirement in the ISO sense. The reclamation is to be noted explicitly. One reason for it is to break the problem that SHACL \"validation\" is, by this specification's standard, verification; using conformance specifically for the correct-construction requirements applied to the record keeping the EPO demands makes it a special case of conformity that covers requirements which might otherwise create confusion in V&V terms." ;
    ogc:verbatim "since conformance is depricated in ISO let's reclaim it for the specific correctly constructed according to shacl requirement and allow conformity to be used to describe fulfillment of a requirement in the ISO sense. its important that we note that we did this. and one of the reasons i do this is to break the problem where shacl \"validation\" is actually \"verification\" by our standard, and use of conformance specifically for satisfying correct construction requirements applied to the record keeping demanded by the EPO allows it to be a special case of conformity that covers requirements which might otherwise create confusion in V&V terms." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Two glossary terms. conformity: adopted, ISO 9000:2026 3.5.9, fulfilment of a requirement. conformance: refined, specializes conformity, anchored to the same clause's Note 1 (the deprecated synonym, reclaimed on purpose), technical binding W3C SHACL section 3.5 Conformance Checking; the machine-checked, correctly constructed record that satisfies the EPO's record-keeping requirements. Prose: conformance for the SHACL check, conformity for fulfilling a requirement; SHACL's word validation is not used for it. Model: ConformityChecker renamed ConformanceChecker. The docs test no longer bans the word." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-18 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "evaluation record: what the EPO binds it to provide" ;
    ogc:problem """Is the evaluation record merely a record in the ISO sense, or is it bound by the EPO to contain what traceability and coverage need, and is that binding checkable.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 13 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:evaluation-record ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-17 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 17 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-18 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The evaluation record in this context is bound by the EPO, which determines what must be present for formalized traceability in both directions and for coverage. The EPO makes all three directly checkable by SHACL over the evaluation record; a conformant evaluation record therefore provides both traceability directions and coverage." ;
    ogc:verbatim "the evaluation record in this context is bound by the EPO, it determines what must be present for formalized traceability (both directions) and coverage. the EPO makes all 3 of those directly checkable via shacl over the evaluation record. thus a conformant evaluation record provides both traceability directions and coverage." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Narrative of evaluation record rewritten to say so; scope note names the two directions (downward flow-down from requirement to criteria to plan to probes, upward derivation from attestation to evidence to probe to criterion to requirement). New shape S2-Requirement: every requirement belongs to the requirement set and has at least one acceptance criterion derived from it, so the downward direction is checked; the upward direction was already checked by S6 and S8." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-19 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "evidence: at what level, and what it maps to" ;
    ogc:problem """The record bound each evidence item to one turn's response and the attestation to the probe. Evidence can also exist at the session level (a trajectory) and rolled up over many sessions (a sensitivity test or robustness battery), so binding to a probe is too narrow; and nothing said what an evidence item is evidence of.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "row 14 of rulings sheet 01" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:objective-evidence ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:expected-results ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-18 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 18 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-19 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Evidence can exist at the probe level, at the session level, and rolled up to a multi-session scenario such as a sensitivity test or a robustness battery. It is therefore not necessarily bound to a probe, though it may be. Evidence must be related to the test plan and in particular maps to an expected result: something that can be ruled on directly as a Boolean, whether it did or did not happen." ;
    ogc:verbatim "evidence can exist at the probe level but it can also exist at a session level, and it can also be rolled up to a multi-session scenario which might be from a sensitivity test or robustness battery. so its not necessarily bound to a probe (though it could be) rather evidence needs to be related to the test plan and in particular it maps to an expected result. the expected result being something that can be ruled on directly as a bool. did it or did it not happen." ;
    ogc:changeNote "EPO: Response (what the system produced at a turn) separated from Evidence; Evidence answers exactly one acceptance criterion's expected result with a Boolean observed value (a determination, ISO 9000:2026 3.11.1), derives from one or more responses or trajectories, is attributed to whoever determined it, and is bound to the test plan; TestSuite (SEVOCAB, altLabel battery) groups sessions for the multi-session level. Attestation binds to evidence; the chain rule now requires every evidence an attestation uses to answer the attested criterion under a plan that has it as an objective. Record: response-1, evidence-1a (a1, observed false), evidence-1b (a2, observed false). Shapes S5-Response, S5-Evidence, S4-TestSuite; S6 chain rule rewritten. Glossary: test suite added; objective evidence narrative rewritten. Model: Response and two Evidence items, level enum, SCI-05 and SCI-06 rewritten." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-19 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 19 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-10 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The recommendation on C-10 is accepted, option (b): the headword is test item (SEVOCAB, ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021, work product to be tested), with system under test, SUT, test object, application and system of interest as alternative labels and the related entries as seeAlso citations. Prose and the model may continue to say system under test." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i accept your recommendation" ;
    ogc:changeNote "The recommendation accepted: headword test item (SEVOCAB, ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2:2021, work product to be tested) with system under test, SUT, test object, application and system of interest as alternative labels; seeAlso system-of-interest (15288), NIST AI 700-2 application, AI-based system (29119-11), object of conformity assessment (ISO/IEC 17000 4.2), and the 14756 system under test entry kept visible as the only standard definition of the phrase. Prose and the model keep saying system under test." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-20 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "evidence and determination: domain and codomain" ;
    ogc:problem """R-18 was implemented with the determination folded into the evidence item as a Boolean observed value. Z, rereading: that is wrong; evidence is the domain and the determination is the codomain, and the determination is not strictly Boolean because EARL admits cantTell.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z rereading the R-18 summary" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:objective-evidence ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:determination ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:sheet-01-walkthrough-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-20 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 20 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-20 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The R-18 implementation is wrong: evidence is the domain and the determination is the codomain. Evidence is collected and serves as the basis for a determination of whether the expected outcome has been met. The determination is not strictly Boolean: per EARL, cantTell is admitted." ;
    ogc:verbatim "this is wrong evidence the domain and the boolean is the codomain. we collect evidence which serves as the basis for a boolean determination regarding whether the expected outcome has been met. furthermore that is not strictly boolean, per earl we admit canttell" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Evidence keeps only what it is: collected material (a response, a trajectory, or several) bearing on one criterion's expected result, under the test plan, attributed to its collector. Determination is a separate node, an earl:Assertion and prov:Activity: it uses one or more evidence items, tests the criterion, and records the EARL outcome passed, failed or cantTell, its mode and its assertor; the paper's word for it is judgment. The attestation aggregates the determinations for a criterion. Glossary: determination becomes a term (ISO 9000:2026 3.11.1, altLabel judgment); objective evidence's narrative corrected. Shapes: S5-Evidence without observed; S6-Determination; the closure rule and chain rule run attestation to determination to evidence. Model: Determination item def replaces the observed attribute." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model revision planning, 2026-09-06 (parties, EPO structure, role of SysML)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Planning the deep revision of the SysML model with Z, 2026-09-06" ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith rul:Z ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-21 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "parties to the evaluation are implicit" ;
    ogc:problem """The model and record name persons on an evaluation team and a system under test but no organizations: nobody sponsors the evaluation, nobody is accountable for the test item, nobody performs the testing as a service, and the affected stakeholders have no place. The wiring cannot guarantee an end state it does not know the parties to.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, entering plan mode for the model revision" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:stakeholder ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:technical-expert ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-21 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 21 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-21 ;
    ogc:rulingText """Explicit stakeholders are introduced. There are affected stakeholders; the organization responsible for the system under test (the test item); the organization performing the testing; and possibly an organization sponsoring the testing that is not the organization operating the test item. Affected stakeholders are populations of actors, whereas the sponsor organization, the testing organization and the organization accountable for the test item are concrete single entities.

The requirements are established and agreed between the sponsor organization and the testing organization; the arrangement is viewed as a contract to perform OG-CAIE as a service. The organization that owns the system under test may or may not be the sponsor, and members of the affected stakeholder classes may or may not be interviewed as part of the requirements development process. Most often the interests of affected stakeholders are represented in the requirements on the basis of input from the domain expert, who is employed in the selection of the domain-specific ontology and in the appropriateness assessment of the requirements; the domain expert may also participate in developing or approving test plans, in the interpretation of the evidence collected, and in attestations to the conformity, or not, of the system under test with regard to those requirements under those test plans. This domain expert role is distinct from the expert role responsible for administering the tests and gathering the evidence per the test plan.

Part definitions and port definitions specify this as an abstract wiring diagram before it is tested by concrete walkthroughs that exercise the specification on a simple demonstration case.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """switched you into plan mode so you can work. note that we want to introduce some explicit stakeholders: there are going to be affected stakeholders and there is going to be the organization responsible for the system under test (test item) and there is the organization performing the testing, there may also be an organization sponsoring the testing who is not the organization operating the test item. effected stakeholders are populations of actors where as the sponsor org, the org doing the testing and the org accountable for the system under test (test item) are concrete single entities. the the requirements are established and agreed upon by the the sponsor org and the org doing the testing (this is viewed as a contract to perform the OG-CAIE as a service), the organization that owns the system under test may or may not be the sponsor and members of the affected stakeholder classes may or may not be interviewed as part of the requirements development process. most often the interests of affected stakeholders are represented in the requirements on the basis of input from the domainexpert who is employed in the selection of the domain specific ontology and in the appropriateness assessment of the requirements; they may also participate in developing or approving test plans, and the interpretation of evidence collected to and attestations to the conformity (or not) of the system under test with regard to those requirements under those test plans. this Domain expert role is distinct from the expert role responsible for administering the tests and gathering the evidence per the test plan.

part def and port def should allow us to specify this in an abstract wiring diagram before we test it out be creating concrete walk throughs that exercise the specification by providing a simple concrete demo case""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Parties become part defs: SponsorOrganization (ISO 9000:2026 customer), TestingOrganization (provider), AccountableOrganization (ISO/IEC 17000 first party), AffectedPopulation with multiplicity 0..*, EvaluationTeam (refined from audit team). A service agreement (ISO 9000:2026 contract) between sponsor and testing organization precedes the requirement set. Populations are interviewed (a StakeholderInput) or represented by a domain expert, and the record says which. The EPO becomes seven steps: agree, scope, declare requirements, plan, execute, determine and attest, report. Glossary: organization, customer, provider, first-, second- and third-party conformity assessment activity, contract, evaluation team added (this slice); model, shapes and record follow in the next slices." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-22 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "which artifact is canonical: the SysML source or its RDF rendering" ;
    ogc:problem """OpenSysML v0.4.3 cannot quantify over collections, so value-level guarantees cannot be written as SysML constraints; and the model's `-convert ttl` rendering is deterministic, uses the OMG SysML vocabulary with stable element IRIs, and is the form Flexo MMS ingests. The question was whether the SysML text or the rendered graph is the canonical structure, and what the SysML is for.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "read-only probes of the toolchain during planning" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-22 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 22 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-22 ;
    ogc:rulingText "SysML is used primarily for part definitions, port definitions and wiring rules: structure only, with value checks living in SHACL. The rendered RDF version of the models is the canonical record, and the SysML source is treated as a view, even where it is the view used for authoring. OpenSysML renders SysML models as RDF triples because it is designed to work with Flexo MMS; the conversion is deterministic and uses the OMG SysML vocabulary with stable IRIs. A parsimony heuristic prunes the TBox not required by the model, following the pattern applied and described in the authors' earlier work." ;
    ogc:verbatim """we're using sysml primarily for part def and port def and wiring rules; we [Structure only: parts, ports, wiring rules; value checks live in SHACL] are use the rendered rdf version of those models for our canonical record and treating the sysml json as a view (even if its a view used for authoring) / opensysml allows us to render our sysml models as rdf triples because its designed to work with flexo mms / "The conversion is deterministic and uses OMG SysML vocabulary with stable IRIs, currently ~1.1 MB" we can use a parsimony heuristic where we prune tbox that is not required for our model. / i believe the parimony heuristic is applied and described in this repo [a local path]""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Four messages joined with a slash. The bracketed clause is the option Z selected when the first message was cut off. SysML holds structure only: part defs, port defs, interface defs, the action def and the assembly; requirement defs and -satisfy leave the model. The pruned RDF rendering of the model (model/og-caie.model.ttl, committed, byte-identical in the gate) is the canonical structure and the SysML source is the authoring view. Wiring rules are SHACL shapes over the model graph; value and provenance rules stay SHACL shapes over the record. Parsimony follows the authors' earlier lifecycle model builds: a term map of the OMG terms kept, a manifest, and a triple budget with a rationale." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-23 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "actor categories on the testing organization's side, and named persons in the demo" ;
    ogc:problem """The plan carried two human roles (domain expert, evaluator) and no signatory for the service agreement; and the worked example used anonymous role-holders (an epidemiologist, a community educator, a red-teamer).""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, rejecting the first submitted plan" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:technical-expert ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-23 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 23 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-23 ;
    ogc:rulingText """A domain expert and an evaluation operator are abstract actor categories that populate distinct slots in the EPO. There is also an account executive on the side of the evaluation organization, who is the signatory on the contract and the legal representative of the organization performing the evaluation.

The worked example names the Humane Intelligence executive (Mala), the domain expert (Annie) and the evaluation operator (Theo). These are references to real people, but the case is synthetic: the people named will recognise their roles, and they are not asked to make attestations within the demonstration case, which is a simplified synthetic walkthrough of the process.

DomainExpert, EvaluationOperator and AccountExecutive are all roles held by members of the company performing the evaluation. Each role must be filled, so there is an at-least-one rule. The account executive is exactly one, being the counterparty who represents the organization in the contract; the domain expert and evaluation operator roles are one or more.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """you need a domain expert and an evaluation operator they are abstract actor categories that populate distinct slots in the EPO. there is also an account executive on the side of the evaluation org who is the signatory on the contract and the legal representative of the org performing the eval. 

our worked example names the HI executive (Mala), the Domain Expert Annie and the eval operator Theo. this are references to real people but the case is synthetic. the real people will read this and appreciate having their roles recognized but are not being asked to make attestations within the demo case which is a simplified synthetic walkthrough of the process / just to clarify "DomainExpert, EvaluationOperator and AccountExecutive" are all members of the company performing the eval. these are roles. the accountexec is limited to cardinality 1 but we allow more domain experts and operators to be on the team. / each role must be filled so there is an at least one rule. this means exec is exactly one since they are the counterparty representing the org in the contract. the the domain expert and eval operator roles are one or more""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Three messages joined with slashes. Three abstract actor categories, each a distinct part def and EPO slot, all held by the testing organization (accountExecutive[1]; team.domainExpert[1..*]; team.operator[1..*]; shape M5-Cardinality): DomainExpert, EvaluationOperator (headword evaluation operator, refined from the SEVOCAB operator; AI evaluation expert becomes its altLabel) and AccountExecutive (signatory and legal representative of the testing organization; no evidence, determination or attestation port). The worked example names Mala (account executive), Annie (domain expert) and Theo (evaluation operator); the record and the record page state that the case is synthetic, that the names recognise the roles of real people, and that no attestation in the record was made by them." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-24 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "contract: no capture of ISO 9000:2026 clause 3.3.13" ;
    ogc:problem """The 37 screenshots skip clauses 3.3.9 to 3.3.15, so the 2026 wording of contract is not held. The glossary entry carries the 2015 wording (3.4.7, "binding agreement") as a pending quote naming the screenshot still to be captured (iso-9000-2026-obp-38). Z: capture 3.3.9 to 3.3.15 and tick sheet 03 row 7.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "clause map of the screenshots during planning" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:contract ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-25 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "first, second and third party: three adopted terms or one refined term" ;
    ogc:problem """ISO/IEC 17000 defines first-, second- and third-party conformity assessment activity as three headwords. The glossary adopts all three verbatim (no coinage); the alternative is one refined term, conformity assessment party, with the three as altLabels, which would be a fourth coinage. Z to confirm the three-term reading or rule otherwise.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "open" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "party anchoring during planning" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:first-party-conformity-assessment-activity ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-26 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "account executive: canonical anchor" ;
    ogc:problem """No ranked source defines account executive. The entry is anchored, provisionally, as a specialization of ISO 9000:2026 3.1.3 top management (the person top management authorizes to sign for the organization); ISO/IEC 17000 4.3 Note 1 (parties to a contract) and 4.6 conformity assessment body are the alternatives. Z to confirm or redirect.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "open" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "R-23 implementation" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:account-executive ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-27 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "port reuse across actor categories: activities confused with actors" ;
    ogc:problem """The first structure-only model let the account executive deliver the report and the recommendation through ports typed by the operator's RecommendationWrite and the calculator's ReportWrite, and the wiring test excused the reuse as by design. Delivering is a different activity from recommending, with its own output.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the T2 wiring test" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:account-executive ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-24 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 24 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-27 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Port sharing is bad practice: it confuses activities with actors. Actors may perform multiple activities, but each activity needs precise inputs and outputs." ;
    ogc:verbatim "port sharing seems like bad practice to me. i think you are confusing activities with actors. actors can perform multiple activities but the activities need precise inputs and outputs" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Every step of the EPO action def now declares its inputs as well as its outputs. Delivery is an item kind of its own (the report and the recommendation handed to the sponsor), the output of the account executive's delivery activity, carried on DeliveryWrite through a delivery seam to the sponsor and a second seam into the record; the sponsor's report and recommendation ports and the executive's reuse of RecommendationWrite and ReportWrite are gone. Shape M5-PortsBelongToRoles now restricts the account executive to AgreementWrite and DeliveryWrite. The one activity two actor categories may both perform is determining on evidence (R-21), so DeterminationWrite is the only supplier port definition carried by two roles, and the wiring test says exactly that." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-28 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "one wire per port, or shared output wires" ;
    ogc:problem """The first wiring rule required every port to be the end of exactly one seam, which forced the recorder to carry six identical record outputs, the probe deriver two probe outputs and the account executive two delivery outputs, one per reader.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the T2 model" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-25 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 25 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-28 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Input wires must be unique. Output wires may be shared, provided that what flows over them is information whose use is nondestructive." ;
    ogc:verbatim "input wires need to be unique, output wires can be shared as long as the thing that flows over those wires is information whose use is nondestructive" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Shape M2-InputsUniqueOutputsShared replaces M2-PortConnectedOnce: every port is wired; an input (conjugated) port is the end of exactly one seam; an output port may feed several. The recorder has one record output read by six, the probe deriver one probe output read by the operator and the recorder, the account executive one delivery output read by the sponsor and the recorder: 47 ports, 27 seams. Everything that flows is information." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-29 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "what the wiring rules are rules over, and what the EPO is as a process" ;
    ogc:problem """The first M-shapes were a mixed bag: some per port, some per interface, one over the package. Z asked for rules that can be checked and explained locally, defined over kinds of parts and kinds of ports; and for the EPO to be read as a process DAG, with loops admitted only if the end state needs them.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the T2 and T3 shapes" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-26 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 26 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-29 ;
    ogc:rulingText """Every wiring must be checkable and explainable locally. At the part level, all of a part's input ports and output ports are examined to ensure that every input is present and every output goes somewhere. At the wire level, each wire is examined for where it comes from and where it goes: an output port on a part to an input port on a part. If all rules are defined over kinds of parts and kinds of ports, then enforcing the rules for all parts and wires yields a well-constructed wiring diagram; ports are how wires are addressed. This is essentially the typed block diagram once directionality and the cardinality rules are enforced: one input wire per port, and output wires that may split as long as they are informational.

In all likelihood the EPO is a process DAG. Loops are allowed if they are needed to guarantee that the final output record has full requirements traceability in both directions and test coverage, computable by SHACL constraints.

The ability to ensure a full record with executable checks on traceability and coverage is precisely what allows the final result to be trusted, and it supports drilling in and deterministically answering the "why" questions related to that result.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """you should be able to check and explain every wiring locally:

* at the part level you look at all its input ports and all its output ports and ensure that all the inputs a present and all the outputs go somewhere
* at the wire level you look at where it came from and where it goes to (output port on part to input port on part)


if all our rules are defined over kinds of parts and kinds of ports then we can get a well constructed wiring diagram from enforce rules for all parts and wires, ports are how we address wires. this is essentially just the typed block diagram once we enforce directionality and the cardinality rules relating input wires to 1 per port and output wires to split as long as they are informational. / in all likelihood our EPO is a process DAG / though i would allow loops if we need them to ensure our final output record is guarantee to have full requirements traceability in both directions and test test coverage, computable by shacl constraints / the ability to ensure we can get a full record with executable checks on traceability and coverage is precisely what allows us to trust the final result. and supports diving in and asking and deterministically answering "why" questions related to that result""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Four messages joined with slashes. Shapes M2-Wire (per wire: from an output port on a part to an input port on a part, one item kind, ends typed by the seam) and M2-Part (per kind of part: every input present on exactly one wire, every output going somewhere) replace the port-targeted shape; M4-ProcessDag checks the succession graph has no cycle (loops stay admissible by ruling if the end state needs them; none does). queries/wiring.rq explains every port locally and renders as a table on the assemblage page. SCI-11 restated." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:R-27 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 27 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-24 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The ISO 9000:2026 clause for contract is 3.3.13. The page carrying clauses 3.3.9 to 3.3.13 is captured and the quote, binding agreement, is verified; sheet 03 row 7 is ticked." ;
