rul:C-31 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "evaluation team anchored to audit team: the word audit reads as legal" ;
    ogc:problem """Z, reviewing sheet 03 row 8: "the word audit feels out of place. most people think of audits and evals differently, as an audit is expected to have legal bearing." The refined term evaluation team is anchored to ISO 9000:2026 3.12.7 audit team ("one or more persons conducting an audit, supported if needed by technical experts"), and technical expert (R-10) to 3.12.9, also marked <audit>. ISO's own definition of audit carries no legal bearing, but readers bring the connotation. Candidate re-anchors: SEVOCAB assessment team, "one or more individuals who jointly perform a process assessment" (ISO/IEC 33001:2015, 3.2.10, p. 30), with audit team as seeAlso; or keep the ISO anchor with a scope note that disclaims the legal reading.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reviewing sheet 03 row 8" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:evaluation-team ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:technical-expert ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-28 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 28 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-31 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Evaluation team is anchored to the SEVOCAB evaluator. The other two candidates, ISO 9000:2026 3.12.7 audit team and SEVOCAB assessment team (ISO/IEC 33001:2015 3.2.10), are kept as seeAlso citations." ;
    ogc:verbatim "go with evaluator but use the see also for the other two." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Evaluation team is now refined from the SEVOCAB evaluator (ISO/IEC 25000:2014, 4.18: individual or organization that performs an evaluation), the same SQuaRE family as the adopted term evaluation; ISO 9000:2026 3.12.7 audit team (pending Z's tick, sheet 03 row 8) and SEVOCAB assessment team (ISO/IEC 33001:2015, 3.2.10) are seeAlso. The label evaluator leaves the evaluation operator's altLabels. Technical expert stays under R-10 with its scope note." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-32 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the front matter does not prepare the audience; the glossary is rendered whole; Popper is everywhere" ;
    ogc:problem """Reading the site after v0.2.0, Z found the front page carried the whole vocabulary discipline, the site map and the offers; the glossary page rendered all 58 terms as one table; the Popperian framing was mixed into the technical pages; and there was no shorthand for the class of evaluation the method is for. The audience is not prepared for the contracting workflow and then the evaluation workflow.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the site in the browser pane" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:og-caie ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:contextual-ai-evaluation ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-29 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 29 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-32 ;
    ogc:rulingText """The Popper concepts connect to the main OG-CAIE ones through a simple crosswalk table from the Popperian conception of science to the project's terms. The entire glossary is not rendered; the glossary navigation approach from the authors' earlier work is adopted; key terms carry mouse-over definitions in MyST.

This plan defers the work through the whole executable EPO specification, with parts, ports and wirings that demonstrably guarantee a scientific record, and the worked health example. The flow for contracting and mapping stakeholders is separated from the flow for performing the evaluation into distinct chapters. All of that remains required, but the glossary is operationalized first and the prose of the first part tightened, so that the audience is prepared to enter the specification for the contracting workflow and then for the evaluation workflow.

Each chapter is carefully scoped and revised one at a time as it is reached, working forward. This breaks the modelling into bounded definitions and checks, lowers the modeller's cognitive overhead, and helps present the model clearly in the MyST site.

Shorthands are provided: DSO for Domain-Specific Ontology, EPO for Evaluation Process Ontology, CAIE for Contextual AI Evaluation, and OG-CAIE for CAIE performed using the EPO and DSO method this document puts forth. That is the full extent of the coinage; everything else is grounded in cited standards and literature. The Popper-to-standards crosswalk exists to set up what would make the evaluation scientific, and the conclusion must be that OG-CAIE encodes exactly what qualifies as scientific. Popper's definitions occur only in the set-up and in the final conclusion: a coordinate transform from the low-dimensional Popperian conception of science to the high-dimensional engineering standards and back, so that the main narrative arc is accessible to less technical readers.

The top-level page is rescoped from why this counts as science onward. The bridge to formal engineering standards for evaluation is the endpoint of the first chapter, which sets up a more rigorous, though not exhaustive, glossary; the glossary in turn supplies the terms needed to express the executable specifications and then to demonstrate their properties.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """i made the small change i wanted and saved it. this editor is definitely going to be useful for fine grained editing. i think you can go ahead and commit my small edit now. then i want to look a little more closely out how we connect the popper concepts to the main OG-CAIE ones. my intuition is as follows:

we a simple cross walk table connecting the Popper conception of science to our terms.

we do not want the entire glossary rendered.

we do want to adopt the glossary navigation technology i built in the mission twin glossary repo.

we do want key terms to have mouse over definitions in myst.

this plan pushes back the work to go through the whole executable EPO spec with parts and ports and wirings that demonstratbly guarantee a scientific record and the worked health example.  i think we will also need to clear break apart the flow for contracting and mapping stakeholders from the flow for performing the evaluation into seperate chapters. we still need all that stuff but based on my reading we need to operationalize the glossary and tighten up the prose on the first part so the audience is prepared to get into the spec for the contracting workflow and then for the evaluation workflow. / let's assume we will carefully scope and revise each chapter one at a time as we reach them. at this moment we're working forward. this will help us break up the modeling acts into bounded definitions and checks. this will lower my own cognitive overhead as the modeler, and help me present the model clearly in the myst site / great. and we also want to provide a short hand: "DSO" for domain specific ontology and "EPO" for evaluation process ontology. "CAIE" for Contextual AI Evaluation and "OG-CAIE" for CAIE performed using the EPO and DSO method we put forth in this document. That covers the extent of our coinage and everything else is grounded in cited standards and literature. Our Popper ~ Standards gross walk exists to set up the "what would make this scientific" and our conclusion needs to be that we have encoded in our OG-CAIE exactly what qualifies as scientific. the trick is that popper definitions occur only in the set up and in the final conclusion. think of this as a coordinate transform trick from the low dimensional Popperian conception of science to the high dimensional engineering standards back to the low dimension Popperian concept of science so the main narrative arc is accessible to less technical persons. / this means the plan needs to rescope the top level page from 1. why this counts as science on. the bridge to formal engineering standards for evaluation is the endpoint of the first chapter which sets us up for a more rigorous glossary (though not exhaustive) that will in turn give us the terms we need to express our executable specifications and then demonstrate their properties.""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Four messages joined with slashes. Slice A of the site restructuring, one chapter at a time, working forward. Coinage becomes exactly four: Contextual AI Evaluation (CAIE) added as a coined term, OG-CAIE redefined as CAIE performed with the EPO and DSO method, DSO and EPO unchanged. The front page runs from why this counts as science to the bridge into the engineering standards and ends at the forward Popper crosswalk (vocabulary/crosswalk.ttl, six rows citing the BoF deck, rendered to generated/popper.md); the conclusion page reads the crosswalk backwards and states that the essentials, shapes and record encode exactly what qualifies as scientific; a test confines the Popperian words to those two pages. The glossary page carries the site map, the discipline, and a hover glossary of exactly the terms the prose references ({term} roles rendered to a {glossary} directive by scripts/render.py); the full table is generated for the paper only. Deferred to later slices, each scoped when reached: the contracting chapter, the evaluation chapter (absorbing the assemblage and record pages), the ogc navigation CLI ported from the authors' earlier glossary work; C-30 stays open." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-33 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the bookends test banned ordinary words" ;
    ogc:problem """The slice-A test confined hypothesis, prediction, auxiliary, evidence and the like to the front page and the conclusion by word, although they are common English; the restriction Z intended is on using them in Popper's sense with Popper's definitions.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the slice-A commit" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-30 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 30 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-33 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Care is needed with the Popperian terms because they are common English words. The restriction is not on using the words but on using them in Popper's sense with Popper's definitions. The discussion of what is science, grounded in Popper, stays in the bookends, since it is the paper's core argument: \"CAIE would be science if it covers these things\", ending with \"OG-CAIE demonstrably covers these things\". The inner sections achieve this through engineering standards. The introduction and conclusion cover why and what; the inner chapters cover what and how in enough detail, and executably, to show both in theory through the executable specification and in practice through the worked example that the bar for science is met." ;
    ogc:verbatim """need to be careful with the popper terms because they are pretty common english words. the restriction isn't on using the word its on using them in Popper sense with the popper definitions. that discussion of what is science, grounded in popper stays in the bookends since its essentially the papers core argument: "CAIE would be science if it covers these things" and ends with demonstrably "OG-CAIE" covers these things. but the inner sections are achieving this via engineering standards. think of the intro and conclusion as covering "why/what" and the inner chapters covering "what/how" in enough detail (and executable) so we can show both in theory using our executable spec and in practice through the worked example that we meet the bar for science.""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "The test now bans only the name (Popper, Popperian) and falsifiability's forms from the inner pages; hypothesis, prediction, auxiliary and evidence are free words there, and the rule about their sense is stated in CLAUDE.md and read, not grepped. The two scrubbed sentences on the record page stay scrubbed: they used the words in Popper's sense." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-34 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the seven steps were not bound to a citation" ;
    ogc:problem """The EPO's seven steps carried labels and rulings but no citation to a documented process model, although the quality-management and systems-engineering canon documents evaluation processes. Without the binding the steps read as invented rather than operationalized.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the front page after slice A" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:evaluation-process-ontology ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-31 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 31 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-34 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The first page, OG-CAIE as an executable specification, is in good shape for now. One thing stands out: the seven steps are not bound to a citation. Given the INCOSE canon and quality management, a process model for evaluation is almost certainly documented already; it is to be cited and the steps matched to its steps, to reinforce that nothing here is invented, only operationalized through executable code." ;
    ogc:verbatim """the first page "OG-CAIE as an executable specification" is in good shape for now.  only one thing stood out to me. our "7 steps" are not bound to a citation. knowing incose canon and quality management there is almost certainly a process model already documented for evaluation and we should be citing that and matching our steps to its steps to further reinforce the fact that we are not making any of this op, we're just operationalizing it via executable code""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Each EPO step in vocabulary/epo.ttl now carries a canonical citation and seeAlso citations: agree, scope and declare requirements match the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15288 acquisition, stakeholder needs definition and system requirements definition processes as SEBoK v2.14 describes them (pp. 353, 549, 557; machine-located); plan, execute and report match the ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-2 test strategy and planning (3.51), test execution (3.40) and test completion (3.25) processes as SEVOCAB defines them (machine-located); determine and attest matches ISO/IEC 17000 7.2 decision with 7.1 review, and Annex A's functions (selection, determination, review, decision and attestation) frame the whole, cited by heading. The EPO term cites 29119-2's test process, SEBoK's four 15288 process groups and 17000 4.1 Note 3. The seven ISO/IEC 17000 quotes are pending on sheet 04. Rendered as a table on the glossary page; `ogc steps` prints it; the citation tests and `ogc verify` cover the steps." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-35 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the contracting lifecycle was absent from the model; the two layers of assumptions were not distinguished" ;
    ogc:problem """Work on an evaluation is contracted before it is performed, and delivered and accepted after, but the model held only the evaluation, with the agreement as its first step. The contract pins the first layer of auxiliary assumptions (the counterparties, the object, the requirements' frame, the method); the evaluation pins the second. The record did not say which was which.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, after R-31" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:contract ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:evaluation-process-ontology ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-32 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 32 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-35 ;
    ogc:rulingText """The contracting lifecycle model receives the same treatment as the evaluation steps. Both cycles are in the paper. The contracting cycle connects to the stakeholders and counterparties and, in this work, pins the first layer of auxiliary assumptions. There must be an explicit separation between the layer of work pinned at the point of the contract and what is pinned and recorded within the scope of an evaluation being performed. Although the evaluation is the main focus, the contract provides axiomatic boundary conditions that bound the scope and also supply assumptions which must be documented for the evaluation to be scientific.

The stakeholder terms already introduced are the actors within that lifecycle, which runs from contracting through delivery on the contract. The lifecycle is not to be invented; it is taken from the relevant engineering standards.

The contracting lifecycle exists because work has been performed, but it was absent from the model. It is made explicit because it bears on the auxiliary assumptions: the record must distinguish what was pinned by the contract from what was decided in the course of the contract's fulfilment.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """i am going to need you to do something similar for the contracting lifecycle model. these two cycle are both in our paper. the contracting one connects to the stakeholders, counterparties and in our work pins the first layer of auxilliary assumptions. there needs to be an explicit seperation from the layer of work pinned at the point of the contract from what is pinned and recorded within the scope of an evaluation being performed. although our evaluation is itself the main focus, we need to acknowledge that the contract provides us axiomatic boundary conditions which served to bound scope but also provide assumptions we must document in order for the evaluation to be scientific. / it has not been written we introduced stakeholder terms that are the actors within that life cycle which covers contracting through delivery on that contract but we do not want to invent this we want to use the relevant engineering standards / the contracting lifecycle exists because work has been performed but it was absent from our model. we're making it explicit because it has bearing on the auxillary assumptions, we need to distinguish in our record what was pinned by the contract from what was decided in the course of the contracts fulfillment""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Three messages joined with slashes. The contracting lifecycle is now explicit: six steps C1 need, C2 propose, C3 agree, C4 access, C5 deliver, C6 acceptDelivery (epo:ContractingStep; accept is a SysML keyword), each citing the canon step it matches: SEBoK's business or mission analysis (p. 543), supplier and acquisition process (pp. 352, 353); ISO/IEC 17000 access (4.10), scheme (4.9) and acceptance (9.6); ISO 9000:2026 contract (3.3.13) and requirement (3.5.1); SEVOCAB test environment (29119-2 3.34), test completion report (3.26) and acceptance (24748-5). The evaluation keeps six steps, scope to report; agree left it. New item kinds Need, Proposal, Acceptance. Every item class declares ogc:pinnedAt epo:contract or epo:evaluation; shape S0-Layers checks that the first layer is closed before the second opens; S0-Need, S0-Proposal and S9-Acceptance check the new items; the record's measles run carries a need, a proposal and an acceptance. The Popper crosswalk's auxiliary-assumption row splits into the two layers; SCI-13 states the separation. The model follows in the same slice: a ContractingProcess action def wrapping the EvaluationProcess." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-36 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "two cycles as two action defs, or one nested model" ;
    ogc:problem """After R-32 the assembly performed two actions side by side, the contracting lifecycle and the evaluation, with the handoff stated only in prose. SysML can nest: an action usage typed by another action def is a black box with defined inputs and outputs, and that action def is its drill-down, conforming to the interface above by typing. Z asked whether OpenSysML gives us that power and, if so, to make it the way the two layers are modeled.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, after R-32" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-33 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 33 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-36 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The two-layer model is to be one model exercising the nesting power of SysML, provided OpenSysML supports it: the outer model treats fulfilment as a black box with well-defined inputs and outputs, and the drill-down specifies that black box in more detail as a specification of its own, which necessarily conforms to the inputs and outputs, and carries the assumptions, of the layer above. This is committed to the plan and to memory as the way the two layers are modelled." ;
    ogc:verbatim """sysml is pretty powerful so one question i have as a relatively new user of opensysml is whether we have the power to demonstrate this is a nested model. that is to say the outer model essentially treats fulfillment as a back box (with well defined inputs and outputs) but then we essential drill down and specify it in more detail making that black box into a spec of its own which necessarily conforms to the inputs and outputs (and carries the assumptions) from the layer about. it is my preference for our two layer model to be one model exercising the nesting power of sysml. is this possible and if so can you make sure this is committed to plan and memory as what we are doing""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Possible, and done. Probed on OpenSysML v0.4.3: `action fulfil : EvaluationProcess;` inside the contracting action def validates strictly and converts to a sysml:ActionUsage typed by the inner def; `flow a.x to b.y` between steps converts to sysml:FlowUsage with two resolvable ends; a flow from an action def's own parameter is refused (both ends need dot notation), so `bind step.param = param;` binds it (sysml:BindingConnectorAsUsage). The contracting lifecycle now owns seven steps, need, propose, agree, access, fulfil, deliver, acceptDelivery; fulfil is the evaluation process as a black box, fed the agreement and the access by flows and feeding the report and the recommendation to deliver; the evaluation process binds its own parameters to its steps and flows its items between them; the assembly performs one action. Shape M4-Nesting checks that fulfil is typed by the evaluation process and that every input of the inner process is fed by a flow from the outer and every output used by one. The prune script resolves flow ends (an inline action resolves against its own parameters) and records them as ogm:flowSource and ogm:flowTarget; FlowUsage, BindingConnectorAsUsage and sysml:references join the term map; the budget rises to 5200 from measurement. Recorded in the plan and in memory as the way the layers are modeled: type a step by an action def, bind its parameters, never restate its interface." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-37 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the site had no stated relation to the model; pages were not paired specification and example" ;
    ogc:problem """The site grew page by page without a stated principle of what each page is a view of, and its pages did not pair the abstract specification with the concrete example. Z replanned it as a presentation layer over a self-documenting model.""" ;
    ogc:severity "H" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the map of the site" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-34 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 34 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-37 ;
    ogc:rulingText """The site is replanned. The MyST site is a presentation layer on top of a rich model that documents itself in RDF. The glossary records the terms, their definitions, their lineages, their cross-relationships and the judgement calls that led to the choice of those definitions. The SysML model covers the actual processes, which are bound to the standards and literature they implement; the processes encoded become executable, so their properties become verifiable. The worked examples demonstrably conform to those processes and produce data that meets the specifications for the wires in the model. The formal properties can be checked (correct wiring, and any rule encoded as SHACL shape conformance), and the examples walk through to make it concrete and intuitive for a human reader. Each page of the MyST site is therefore a curated view of the underlying model. The updated outline delivers a story arc that uses the Popper bookends to make this intuitive for a general audience, then inside uses the formal engineering standards for contracting and for quality evaluation processes as a nested SysML model, rendered as RDF and enriched with the glossary content to ensure citation traceability; ultimately guaranteeing an auditable record of the AI evaluation done using OG-CAIE, by encoding and enforcing a DSO-conditioned EPO, and concluding that this record meets Popper's bar for being scientific. The task builds out what the model (representation) and the site (presentation) each must do. The separation of representation and presentation is one of the main value propositions of doing this with GitHub, MyST, Jupyter, Python, OpenSysML, rdflib, pySHACL and other executable systems engineering tools, which allow text-based standards to be codified in a formal record with machine-provable traceability in both directions and coverage.

Eight pages, as listed; each page includes both the formal abstract specification and the relevant concrete example, the two running in parallel throughout, with a recognisable pattern so that the reader can find them. There is always more: highlighting the separation principle makes clear that the presentation layer (the site) is a view rather than the model itself (the repository).

The presentation is calibrated for a human reader, so the texture and the volume of the content are tuned to human sensibilities. The representation layer is made further accessible to AI through CLI tools and skills, as was done for the glossary. In practice the views are tailored to carry the most important information for the human reader, and the concrete examples are made didactic relative to the abstract concepts; the two always appear as a pair.""" ;
    ogc:verbatim """i am thinking its a good time to replan the site. one way to think about it, is that the myst site is just a presentation layer on top of a rich model which documents itself via rdf. the glossary painstakingly records our terms, their definitions, their lineages, their cross relationships and and the judgement calls that lead to the choice of these definitions. the sysml model covers the actually processes which are also painstakingly bound to the standards and literature they implement. the processes we're encoding become executable so their properties become verifiable. our worked examples demonstrably conform to those processes and produce data that meets the specs for the wires in the model. the formal properties can be checked (eg correct wiring / any rule encoded as shacl shape conformance can be checked) AND the examples walk through to make it concrete and ituitive for a human reader. thus each page in the myst site therefore is a curated view of the underlying model. i want you to plan an updated outline for delivering a story arc that balances that uses the Popper bookends to make this intuitive for a general audience, then inside uses the formal engineering standards for contracting and for quality evaluation processes as a nested sysml model, rendered as RDF and enriched with the glossary content to ensure citation traceability. ultimately guaranteeing that we can produce an auditable record of the AI eval done using OG-CAIE (by encoding and enforcing a DSO conditioned EPO), and that this record meets Poppers bar for being scientific (conclusion). this task is essentially to build out what our model (representation) and site (presentation) each must do. the seperation of representation and presentation is one of the main value propositions of doing this via github with myst, jupyter, python, opensysml, rdflib, pyshacl and other executable systems engineering tools that allow us to codify the text based standards in a formal record with machine provable traceability (both directions) and coverage. / yes, 8 pages as listed but make sure pages include both formal abstract specs and the relevant concrete examples. we can think of the formal spec and the example demonstating it as running parallel throughout. it makes sense to establish some patterning so this is easy to recognize for the reader. furthermore, there is always "more" by highlighting the separation principle we can be clear that the presentation layer (the site) is a view rather than the model itself (the repo). / looks very good. just adding that the presentation is calibrated for a human reader so the texture of the content and the volume of the content must be tuned to human sensibilities. the representation layer can be made further accesable to AI via cli tools and skills like we did with the glossary. in practice this means that we need to tailor our views to include the most important information to the human reader, and making concrete examples didactic relative to the abstract concepts. they should always appear as a pair.""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Three messages joined with slashes. The plan of record (2026-09-06) states what the representation must hold and prove and what each of eight pages shows and is rendered from: Why this counts as science, The vocabulary, Contracting, The evaluation, The nested model, What the record proves (executed live in the build), Rulings, Conclusion. Every chapter from Contracting on follows five titled blocks in order (What the standards say; The specification; The walkthrough; Checked; There is more in the model), enforced by a test, with a per-page prose budget. Slice B lands the Contracting chapter: renderer slices by item kind and by step, page tags on the essentials, and rulings sheet 05, generated from the model graph, for Z's block-by-block and wire-by-wire validation (C-30)." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-38 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "what happens after acceptance: narration, not a step" ;
    ogc:problem """The contracting walkthrough ended at acceptance. In practice the sponsor tells the accountable organization to change the system per the findings, the change is made, and the sponsor may require new testing to certify it. Whether to model that or narrate it was open.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the Contracting chapter" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-35 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 35 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-38 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The walkthrough may describe two further steps that are explicitly not part of the contract lifecycle and are identified instead as observed in practice: the county health office tells the chatbot provider to implement changes that result in demonstrable behaviour change per the findings of Humane Intelligence, and the chatbot provider implements the changes, at which point the county health office may require new testing to certify that the flagged issues were addressed. This is not claimed to be in the contracting loop of the standard contracting process; it is an opportunity to show how the evaluation results in demonstrable system behaviour change. For safety-critical deployments these evaluations would be expected to be paired with a release. Since the sponsor organization and the chatbot provider are both already in the example, this is primarily narration, to help the reader understand what the process is like in practice and why it is not an academic exercise." ;
    ogc:verbatim """our walk through can describe an extra step 2 steps which are explicitly not part of the contract lifecycle but should instead be identified as observed in practice:

* the county health office tells the chatbot provider they need to implement changes that result in demonstrable behavior change per the findings of Humane Intelligence and the chatbot provider implements changes (at which point the county heath office may require new testing to certify the flagged issues were addressed).


note that we are not trying to say this is in the contracting loop per the standard contracting process but it is an opportunity to identify how this results in demonstrable system behavior change. for safety critical deployments we'd expect these evaluations to be paired with an release. importantly we already have both the sponsor org and the chatbot provider in our example so this is primarily narration feedback to help the reader understand what this is like in real life and why its not just an academic exercise""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Narration only: a paragraph in the Contracting chapter's walkthrough block, after the record rows, marked as observed in practice and deliberately outside the lifecycle; no step, item or shape added. The standards' word for the repeat, surveillance (ISO/IEC 17000 8.1), is quoted and cited as a seeAlso on the contracting lifecycle class, pending Z's tick on sheet 04 row 12." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-39 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "surveillance as a headword raises eyebrows" ;
    ogc:problem """R-35 quoted ISO/IEC 17000 8.1 surveillance in the Contracting walkthrough. The clause is the formal doctrine for a repeat evaluation and must stay mapped, but the word reads badly to a general audience.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the R-35 commit" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:monitoring ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-36 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 36 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-39 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The headword surveillance would raise eyebrows and is replaced. The citation to ISO/IEC 17000 8.1 surveillance stays mapped to the concept, since it is the formal doctrine on the subject, but an alternative headword is chosen, monitoring or oversight." ;
    ogc:verbatim """"surveillance" might cause some eyebrows to raise. we do need to citation to map to this concept since its the formal doctrine on the subject but we may need to choose an alternative headword. possibly monitoring or oversight.""" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Headword monitoring, adopted from ISO 9000:2026 3.11.3 (determining the status of a system, a process or an activity; Note 3: carried out at different stages or at different times), which outranks 17000 by the precedence rule; surveillance (ISO/IEC 17000 8.1) is the alternative label with its clause and a seeAlso citation, so the doctrine stays mapped. Oversight is not a term of either standard. The walkthrough prose says monitoring; both quotes pending on sheet 04 (rows 12, 13)." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-40 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "no seam carried the sponsor's obligations towards the affected populations" ;
    ogc:problem """The contracting slice of the wiring carried the need, the proposal, the agreement, the access, the delivery and the acceptance, but nothing about what the sponsor owes the affected populations: its expressed mission, duties and obligations, which is the context the contract answers to.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the contracting wiring diagram" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:mission ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-37 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 37 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-40 ;
    ogc:rulingText "An extra seam is added to account for any data covering the obligations, duties or expressed mission the sponsoring organization has with regard to the affected populations. This is context that informs what goes into the contract." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i think we want to add in an extra seem here. specifically we want to account for any data covering what obligations, duties or expressed mission the sponsoring or has with regards to the affected populations. this is context which will inform what goes into the contract" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Item kind Mission (ISO 9000:2026 3.4.11 mission, with 3.4.5 policy as neighbour; glossary term mission, pending Z's tick), pinned at the contract, produced at the need step before the need, regarding one or more affected populations. Seams missionSeam (sponsor to recorder) and missionToExecutiveSeam (sponsor to account executive), the sponsor's one output shared; flows carry it into propose and agree; shape S0-Mission checks it is the sponsor's, names the populations, and that the need is stated under it. The measles record opens with the county office's mission; SCI-10 says it; the crosswalk's first layer names it; the Contracting chapter says it in the standards block, the specification and the walkthrough." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-41 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the mission's relation to the affected populations was in the record but not in the model or the view" ;
    ogc:problem """The record said which populations the sponsor's mission regards, but the SysML model held no relation between the sponsor and the affected populations, and the contracting wiring diagram drew none. Diagrams were also growing dense: one edge per seam, named by the seam, with no statement of what the view brings into focus or leaves out.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the contracting wiring diagram after the mission seam landed" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:mission ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-38 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 38 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-41 ;
    ogc:rulingText "Some connectivity should show how the mission of the sponsor organization relates to the affected population; it may be covered in the data but not in the view. Diagrams tend to be overloaded, so in practice they must stay simple, even if some wires are braided into bundles, since the information flows together in practice. Diagrams are important views for humans, so their density must be balanced against the point they make. As long as the code that produces a diagram reads from the model itself and the perspective the view encodes is documented (what it brings into focus and what it leaves out), any view that is compelling narratively is permitted. The view code is hardened by building reusable views and committing the methods with a CLI and skills for their use, which makes other views possible even though the presentation layer curates." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i still think there should be some kind of connectivity showing how the mission of the sponsor org relates to the affected population, maybe that's covered in the data just not in the view. i know the diagramming is tricky because we tend to overload our diagrams. in practice we need to keep them simple even if it means some wires a braided up into bundles since the information flows together in practice. the diagrams a pretty important views for humans so we need to balance their density with the point they are making. as long as the code that produces the diagram reads from the the model itself and we document the perspective the view encodes (what it brings into focus and what it leaves out) then i think we can do whatever views are compeling narrative wise. plus we can always harden the view code by building resuable views and committing the methods with cli and skills for their use. this makes other views possible even though our presentation layer curates" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Model: connection def Obligation (sponsor end, population end) and the assembly's connection obligation from sponsor to affected; the Mission item owns ref part regards : AffectedPopulation[1..*], which the record's epo:regards binds to; derived ogm:relatesFrom and ogm:relatesTo; shape M1-Obligation with the counterexample no-obligation; budget 5500. Views: registry ogc/views.py (layers, assemblage, contracting, evaluation), each with a title, what it brings into focus and what it leaves out; wires between the same two parts braid into one bundle labelled by the item kinds in the order the process produces them; relations dotted; the site's figures render from the registry with the perspective as caption; ogc views and ogc view <name>; skill updated; sheet 05 gains a Relations table." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-42 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "every chapter says there is more in the model, but a human reader has only the files and the CLI to find it" ;
    ogc:problem """The site curates; block 5 of every chapter points to the repository and to ogc. A human reader without a shell has no interface to the model behind the pages, and the prose grows to compensate.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, after the views landed" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-39 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 39 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-42 ;
    ogc:rulingText "A knowledge graph explorer is added as an appendix, modelled after the authors' earlier d3 work in several repositories. Since the text keeps saying there is more, it is easier to cut the text and link to the explorer. Given the extent of the OWL, SHACL and SPARQL work and the existing machine navigation tools, the human-facing graph exploration interface belongs in the appendix; it must back onto the RDF model (Oxigraph may be used for speed). The work may proceed in parallel with the main body. The appendix is an alternative user interface, not new content." ;
    ogc:verbatim "one other idea i have which i think is interesting would be to put a knowledge graph explorer as an appendix. this would be modeled after work we've done in d3 for several other repos. since we keep saying \"there is more\" it might be easiest to cut down on text and just provide links to the knowledge graph explorer. you have variants in commander, in dsg-ant-private and likely other repos. since we're doing lots of owl, shacl sparql work and we already have the machine navigation tools, it would be good to create the human facing graph exploration interface as the appendix. noting this should backup to the rdf model (you can use oxigraph if it helps with speed). ideally you can get this going in parallel with subagents so we can keep working on the main body. the nice thing about this approach to the appendix is that its an alternative user interface not new content" ;
    ogc:changeNote "An appendix page with a d3 knowledge graph explorer rendered deterministically from the RDF (vocabulary, sources, rulings, essentials, shapes, crosswalk, process, model graph, measles record), each view with its perspective, every node naming the ogc command that prints it; served as static files next to the site; block 5 of every chapter links to it. Built on the branch explorer-appendix, merged after the gate." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .



rul:C-43 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the S-shapes judge the items that exist; no shape requires a step's output to exist" ;
    ogc:problem """The executor's mutations show that a record without a plan approval, or without an access grant, conforms to S0 to S9: the shapes constrain a plan approval or an access grant once present but do not require one. Completeness against the model catches both, and the missing access also empties the traceback. Question for Z: should the S-shapes require the existence of each step's outputs (a test plan approved by a domain expert before any session; an access grant behind every session), or is completeness against the model the right place for existence, keeping the shapes local to the items?""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "open" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "the executor's mutation table (ogc/executor.py), while building the guarantees chapter" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-44 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "ogc has no reader for the record: explorer nodes for record items point at their class, not the item" ;
    ogc:problem """The knowledge graph explorer names, on every node, the ogc command that prints it. For record items (track/measles-run.ttl) there is none: the record is not among the files ogc loads, so the node points at a DESCRIBE of the item's class. A --record flag on ogc sparql, or a reader of the record, would close this.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "open" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "building the explorer appendix (R-39)" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-45 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the model counterexample no-obligation also fires M5-Cardinality" ;
    ogc:problem """counterexamples/model/no-obligation.sysml is built to fail M1-Obligation and does, but its bare testing organization declares no account executive, so M5-Cardinality fires too; the test only asks that the named shape is among those fired. Either the counterexample gains an account executive so that it fails M1-Obligation alone, or the claim is read as sufficiency.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "open" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "the contracting proof notebook, which prints every shape a counterexample fires" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:C-46 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the sponsor's decision to interview or represent each affected population had no item of its own" ;
    ogc:problem """The record showed one population interviewed and one represented, and S0-Population accepted either, but nothing said who decided which, or that the decision was a scope-of-work judgment made with the contract; the evaluation's scope step had no input carrying it.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the contracting walkthrough" ;
    ogc:concernsTerm term:statement-of-work ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-40 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 40 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-46 ;
    ogc:rulingText "In the example, one of the boundary items between the contract and the fulfilment of the evaluation contract covers the sponsor's decision that the commuters needed to be interviewed while the residents could simply be represented. This is the kind of judgement call that affects the scope of work: whether expert representation is accepted or interviews and documented stakeholder-needs input are required. The example case splits across two affected stakeholder groups to show that both options are valid and traceable under the EPO. In practice representation is the most common, with stakeholder interviews reserved for extenuating circumstances because of the increased level of effort, generally for under-represented stakeholders or under-documented stakeholder needs." ;
    ogc:verbatim "in the example one one of the boundary items between the contract and the fulfillment of the evaluation contract should cover the sponsors decision that the commuters needed to be interviewed but that the residents could simply be represented. this is the kind of judgement call that effects scope of work (whether or not we accept expert representation or require interviews and documented stakeholder needs input). in our example case we split across two affected stakeholder groups so we can show that both options are valid and traceable under out EPO. in practice the most common is represented with stakeholder interviews reserved for extenuating circumstances due to the increase in level of effort. generally reserved for underrepresent stakeholders or underdocumented stakeholder needs." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Item kind StatementOfWork (SEVOCAB statement of work, p. 406; glossary term statement of work with altLabels SOW and scope of work), pinned at the contract, produced at the agree step by the sponsor, carried by two seams (to the recorder and to the account executive) and by a flow into the evaluation process's scope step; one engagement decision per affected population, interview or representation (enum Engagement; epo:decides, epo:population, epo:engagement). Shape S0-StatementOfWork; S0-Population now requires the decision and that the record realizes it. The measles record: commuters interviewed, residents represented, by the county's decision on 31 July. Executor: the agree template emits it; mutation engagement-mismatch is caught by S0-Population; counterexample engagement-mismatch.ttl. Contracting chapter says it in the specification and the walkthrough." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-47 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the rulings sat among the chapters as a page most readers will not want" ;
    ogc:problem """The rulings page was the seventh page of the main path. It is an important record, but it interrupts the arc for a reader who does not need it, and its place in the account of the method (the subjective choices behind the objective results) was not stated.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the site after the two appendices landed" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-41 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 41 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-47 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The rulings move to an appendix. The concept is important, but most readers will not need it. The rulings are already part of the knowledge graph explorer; they fit best as Appendix C. The knowledge graph explorer allows navigation of the whole model and the computational proofs demonstrate the guarantees the model can prove computationally; Appendix C is the acknowledgement of the subjective choices, judgements and interpretations the model is grounded in. These subjectivities are implicit in the objective results the computational proofs produce, and the claim that this is scientific holds with these judgements as auxiliary assumptions. Appendix C is framed as the specification holding itself to its own definition of science." ;
    ogc:verbatim "i think i want ruling moved to an appendix. it is an important concept but the truth is most readers won't care. the rulings are already part of the knowledge graph explorer but i think they fit better as appendix C. since the knowledge graph explorer is allowing navigation of the whole model. the computational proofs demonstrate the guarantees or model can actually prove computationally, this would make appendix C the acknowledgement of the subjective choices, judgements and interpretations that model is grounded in. these subjectivities are implicit in the objective results produced by the computational proofs, and the claim that this is scientific holds with these judgements as auxilliary assumptions. we can frame appendix C as holding ourselves to our own definition of science." ;
    ogc:changeNote "docs/rulings.md becomes Appendix C, last in the toc after Appendix B, framed as Z says: the subjective choices the model is grounded in, implicit in the computational proofs, held as the auxiliary assumptions of the claim to science. The main path is seven pages; the vocabulary page's map and the README say so." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-48 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the outline's page names did not tell a skimming reader what the site contains" ;
    ogc:problem """Glossary, Contracting, The evaluation, What the record proves: each named a page after its device rather than its subject, so the outline read as a list of parts, not as what a reader would find.""" ;
    ogc:severity "L" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, reading the outline after Appendix C" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-42 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 42 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-48 ;
    ogc:rulingText "The page names are tightened: Glossary becomes Standards and Definitions; Contracting becomes Stakeholders and Contracting; The evaluation becomes Context and Evaluation; What the record proves becomes Records and Reporting; Conclusion stays. This gives a reader who skims the outline a much clearer picture of what the site contains. The appendices provide the first rung of backup and the repository as a whole an even richer one, so the reader need not take the authors on trust: the reader can verify." ;
    ogc:verbatim "we can also tighten up naming: Glossary -> Standards and Definitions; Contracting -> Stakeholders and Contracting; The evaluation -> Context and Evaluation; what the record proves -> Records and Reporting; Conclusion. i think this gives a reader who skims the outline a much clearer picture of the website contains. the appendices provide the first rung of backup and the repo as a whole provides an even richer backup. so the reader doesn't need to just trust us. they can verify themselves" ;
    ogc:changeNote "Page titles renamed as Z lists them, in the site's sentence case; file names and links unchanged. The nested model keeps its name." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-49 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the toolchain that makes the specification executable was nowhere reviewed for the reader" ;
    ogc:problem """The site says it is executable and the gate proves it, but no page tells a reader what the environment is, what is pinned, and how to reproduce the checks; without it the computational paradigm reads as another document.""" ;
    ogc:severity "M" ;
    ogc:status "ruled" ;
    ogc:surfacedOn "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ogc:surfacedHow "Z, after Appendix C" ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy rul:model-revision-planning-2026-09-06 .

rul:R-43 a ogc:Ruling ;
    ogc:order 43 ;
    ogc:resolves rul:C-49 ;
    ogc:rulingText "An Appendix D, Toolchain and Reproducibility, is added: a detailed review of the toolchain from virtual environments to the packages used, modelled after the portion of the SciPy proceedings supplementary material that covers the same subject. The reader should see that this is a computational paradigm, not just another document." ;
    ogc:verbatim "and i think we could even add an Appendix D which provides a detailed review of our toolchain from virtual environments to packages we used. \"Appendix D: Toolchain and Reproducibility\" modeled after the portion of the scipy-proceedings suplemental materials that covered the same subject. i am really pushing for the reader to see this is a computational paradigm not just another document." ;
    ogc:changeNote "Appendix D, toolchain and reproducibility, after Appendix C: rendered from the lock file, the pinned converter digests, the gate script and the CI workflow, never typed by hand; a reviewer recipe modeled on the SciPy proceedings supplementary material (uv sync, the gate, ogc doctor, the site)." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo rul:Z ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-06"^^xsd:date .

rul:C-50 a ogc:Concern ;
    rdfs:label "the nested lifecycle page was named for the model and did not say that the record rests on both cycles" ;
    ogc:problem """The page was titled The nested model; and though the record hub receives items from the contracting lifecycle and from the evaluation, the page did not state that the records and the reporting rely on information produced during both.""" ;
